[[compiler-serialization]]
=== Serialization

[abstract]
Serialization placement runs on each pool's tree after segmentation
(<<compiler-calls>>) and before ownership planning (<<compiler-ownership>>).
It decides where a value changes between its native form and its serialized
form, and for each type and language it builds a *serialization plan*: the
schema string, the native type, and the conversions a value needs at a
crossing. Each pool writes the same voidstar layout for a type
(<<build-voidstar>>), so two pools agree on a value with no negotiation.

**Two states.** Inside a pool, every value is either *native* (an object of
the pool's language) or *serialized* (a data packet, <<build-packets>>). An
argument arrives serialized; a result and an argument to a foreign call leave
serialized. The pass gives every subexpression one of the two states and
inserts a transition only where a use needs the other one. An argument is
decoded at most once, at the entry of the function that receives it, and
only if some use needs its native value. A value a pool only passes on to
another pool stays a packet and is never decoded. This is also how a pool
carries a value of a type it cannot hold, such as a record with no native
form in a compiled language: the packet passes through unchanged.

**The plan.** For a type in a language, the plan is a tree with one node per
type constructor. Each node records the wire shape (primitive, list, tuple,
record, enum, variant, optional, table, stream handle, function) and the
language's native name for the constructor at that point. Three things are
read off one plan, so they cannot disagree:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Output | Use
| Schema with hints | Given to the pool's binding, which builds the named native container (`<dict>` in Python) while reading or writing the layout.
| Schema without hints | Stored in packets written to disk and in the manifest. Hints never change the layout, so this form is the same in every language.
| Native type and conversions | The type the generated code declares, and the `Packable` calls placed before writing and after reading.
|===

Aliases are expanded to the type they name, which gives the layout; an
alias's own native form, if it has one, supplies the hint. A built-in width
such as `I64` stops the expansion, so an alias of `Int` keeps the width it
names. A `data` type with no fields in any constructor is an enum; one with
fields is a variant whose arms are built from the declaration, in
declaration order, since the ordinal is the wire tag.

**Newtypes and `Packable`.** A newtype crosses in the form of its
representation (<<newtype>>). Where the language's native form for the
newtype is the representation's form, or a list or tuple container the
binding can build directly, no conversion is needed. Otherwise the plan
routes through a `Packable` instance (<<packable>>): the instance
`Packable W N` gives `unpack :: N -> W` and `pack :: W -> N`, where `W` is
the newtype's representation with its parameters substituted. A sender
calls `unpack` and writes `W`; a receiver reads `W` and calls `pack`. An
intrinsic that acts on the value as it crosses (`@save`, `@show`, `@hash`)
also unpacks first, and one that yields a value read from data (`@load`,
`@read`) packs its result. A record or `data` type has a wire form of its
own and never takes a `Packable` instance.

**Choosing and checking instances.** In each language the plan takes the
most specific instance whose head covers the use site; two equally specific
candidates are an ambiguity error, and an instance with no implementation in
the language that needs it is an error naming that language. Across the
program, every pair of instances whose heads can be instantiated to a common
type must give it the same wire form, or the program is rejected. Because
the wire form is fixed by the newtype's declaration, the instance chosen in
one language and the instance chosen in another always write and read the
same layout.

**Function values.** A function crosses by defunctionalization. Every
function value that may cross is a generated function with a number in its
home pool, applied to zero or more captured values. Its wire form does not
depend on its signature: the tuple of the home language's name, the
function number, and the captured values as self-contained packets, schema
`t3sjaau1`. Captured values are embedded in their packets rather than
referenced in shared memory, because the closure may be applied after the
dispatch that built it has released its blocks.

[mermaid]
....
sequenceDiagram
    participant A as Pool A (home)
    participant B as Pool B
    A->>B: call g, argument f as (A, n, [captures])
    Note over B: reflect: f becomes a native callable
    B->>A: call function n with captures ++ arguments
    A-->>B: result packet
    Note over B: if B passes f on, it sends the original tuple
....

The receiving pool *reflects* the tuple into a native callable of the right
arity, using the argument and result plans the closure's type gives. Calling
it sends a call to the home pool with the captured packets followed by the
new arguments. A reflected closure remembers its origin, so passing it on or
back sends the original tuple, never a chain of forwarding closures. A
function value can be a field of a record or an element of a list; the
enclosing value's layout carries the tuple in that slot. A suspension
`<E> T` crosses the same way, as a closure of no arguments, and is never run
by the crossing (<<compiler-effects>>). A callable that host code created
has no number to call back into: it is refused where the compiler can see
it and fails the call otherwise. A function or suspension has no file form,
so the data-writing and data-reading intrinsics refuse types that contain
one.

**Recursive types.** While building a plan the compiler keeps the set of
type instantiations on the path from the root. Meeting one of them again
ends the descent with a back-reference node. In the schema, the enclosing
node is declared with `&` and a name, and the occurrence refers to it with
`^` (<<build-voidstar>>). The key is the whole instantiation, not the
name: a `Tree Int` inside a `Tree Str` is a different type and is expanded
on its own. A plan whose expansion does not terminate is rejected. The
layout stays finite because a variant's payload, an optional and an array
are behind pointers.

**Tables and handles.** A table's plan is a table node listing its declared
columns, written `T` or `T:` followed by the columns. The value travels as
an Arrow block rather than voidstar, and the declared columns are a lower
bound checked when the block is received (<<build-tables>>). A stream or
file handle travels as the file's path, and the receiving pool opens its
own handle on it (<<build-streaming>>).

**Strings.** Where the receiving language cannot hold a NUL byte in a
string, the generated code checks received strings for one (<<nul-bytes>>).
The check is emitted only for types that contain a string, so other types
pay nothing.

